153:   private static final class LegacyAsyncDispatcher extends Dispatcher {
154: 
155:     // This dispatcher matches the original dispatch behavior of AsyncEventBus.
156:     //
157:     // We can't really make any guarantees about the overall dispatch order for this dispatcher in
158:     // a multithreaded environment for a couple of reasons:
159:     //
160:     // 1. Subscribers to events posted on different threads can be interleaved with each other
161:     //    freely. (A event on one thread, B event on another could yield any of
162:     //    [a1, a2, a3, b1, b2], [a1, b2, a2, a3, b2], [a1, b2, b3, a2, a3], etc.)
163:     // 2. It's possible for subscribers to actually be dispatched to in a different order than they
164:     //    were added to the queue. It's easily possible for one thread to take the head of the
165:     //    queue, immediately followed by another thread taking the next element in the queue. That
166:     //    second thread can then dispatch to the subscriber it took before the first thread does.
167:     //
168:     // All this makes me really wonder if there's any value in queueing here at all. A dispatcher
169:     // that simply loops through the subscribers and dispatches the event to each would actually
170:     // probably provide a stronger order guarantee, though that order would obviously be different
171:     // in some cases.
